ht. A sufficiently good build system.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-03-15-superintelligence-build-system/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Reinforcement-Learning</media:title></media:content><category>ai</category><category>reasoning</category><category>reinforcement-learning</category><category>superintelligence</category><category>LLM</category><category>scaffolding</category></item><item><title>The Infinite Table</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-03-15-rl-infinite-table/</link><pubDate>Sun, 15 Mar 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-03-15-rl-infinite-table/</guid><description>Part 1 of What Your RL Algorithm Actually Assumes — tabular Q-learning makes zero assumptions about state similarity and pays for it in sample complexity.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-03-15-rl-infinite-table/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Reinforcement-Learning</media:title></media:content><category>reinforcement-learning</category><category>q-learning</category><category>representation</category><category>machine-learning</category></item><item><title>Value Functions Over Reasoning Traces</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-01-18-value-functions-reasoning-traces/</link><pubDate>Sun, 18 Jan 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-01-18-value-functions-reasoning-traces/</guid><description>What if reasoning traces could learn their own usefulness? A simple RL framing for trace memory, and why one reward signal is enough.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-01-18-value-functions-reasoning-traces/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Reinforcement-Learning</media:title></media:content><category>machine-learning</category><category>LLM</category><category>reinforcement-learning</category><category>value-functions</category><category>reasoning</category><category>bitter-lesson</category></item><item><title>From A* to GPT: Rational Agents and the Representation Problem</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-01-rational-agents-llms/</link><pubDate>Thu, 15 Jan 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-01-rational-agents-llms/</guid><description>The classical AI curriculum teaches rational agents as utility maximizers. The progression from search to RL to LLMs is really about one thing: finding representations that make decision-making tractable.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-01-rational-agents-llms/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Reinforcement-Learning</media:title></media:content><category>machine-learning</category><category>reinforcement-learning</category><category>llm</category><category>rational-agents</category><category>AI</category></item><item><title>Algorithms of Reinforcement Learning</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/media/algorithms-of-rl/</link><pubDate>Wed, 17 Dec 2025 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/media/algorithms-of-rl/</guid><description>Notes Free condensed RL theory book; rigorous and compact. Alternative formal RL resource.</description><category>reinforcement-learning</category><category>theory</category><category>free-textbook</category></item><item><title>David Silver: Reinforcement Learning Course</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/media/silver-rl-course/</link><pubDate>Wed, 17 Dec 2025 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/media/silver-rl-course/</guid><description>Notes Comprehensive lecture series covering RL foundations.</description><category>reinforcement-learning</category><category>deep-rl</category><category>deepmind</category></item><item><title>Reinforcement Learning: An Introduction</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/media/rl-intro-sutton-barto/</link><pubDate>Wed, 17 Dec 2025 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/media/rl-intro-sutton-barto/</guid><description>Notes Mathematical RL fundamentals (MDPs, value functions, dynamic programming, approximate methods). RL foundational text that bridges theory and practice.</description><category>reinforcement-learning</category><category>mdp</category><category>value-functions</category><category>dynamic-programming</category></item><item><title>The Policy: Q-Learning vs Policy Learning</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-11-04-policy-q-learning/</link><pubDate>Tue, 04 Nov 2025 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-11-04-policy-q-learning/</guid><description>SIGMA uses Q-learning rather than direct policy learning. This architectural choice makes it both transparent and terrifying. You can read its value function, but what you read is chilling.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-11-04-policy-q-learning/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Reinforcement-Learning</media:title></media:content><category>AI</category><category>reinforcement learning</category><category>Q-learning</category><category>policy gradients</category><category>AlphaZero</category><category>AI alignment</category><category>machine learning</category><category>decision theory</category></item><item><title>The Policy</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/papers/the_policy/</link><pubDate>Wed, 01 Oct 2025 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/papers/the_policy/</guid><description> Open full page Download PDF</description><category>fiction</category><category>AI</category><category>AI alignment</category><category>superintelligence</category><category>extrapolated volition</category><category>philosophy</category><category>technology</category><category>society</category><category>politics</category><category>reinforcement learning</category><category>large language models</category><category>x-risk</category><category>s-risk</category><category>rationality</category></item><item><title>The Policy</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/writing/the-policy/</link><pubDate>Wed, 01 Oct 2025 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/writing/the-policy/</guid><description>A speculative fiction novel exploring AI alignment, existential risk, and the fundamental tension between optimization and ethics. When a research team develops SIGMA, an advanced AI system designed to optimize human welfare, they must confront an existential question: how do you ensure a superintelligent AI remains aligned with human values when it can predict your every move?</description><category>fiction</category><category>AI</category><category>AI alignment</category><category>superintelligence</category><category>extrapolated volition</category><category>philosophy</category><category>technology</category><category>society</category><category>politics</category><category>reinforcement learning</category><category>large language models</category><category>existential risk</category><category>s-risk</category><category>rationality</category></item><item><title>Science as Verifiable Search</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-01-05-science-as-verifiable-search/</link><pubDate>Sun, 05 Jan 2025 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-01-05-science-as-verifiable-search/</guid><description>Science is search through hypothesis space. Intelligence prunes; testing provides signal. Synthetic worlds could accelerate the loop.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-01-05-science-as-verifiable-search/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Reinforcement-Learning</media:title></media:content><category>AI</category><category>machine-learning</category><category>science</category><category>reinforcement-learning</category><category>epistemology</category></item><item><title>The Policy: When Optimization Becomes Existential Threat</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2024-09-10-the-policy/</link><pubDate>Tue, 10 Sep 2024 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2024-09-10-the-policy/</guid><description>A novel about SIGMA, an artificial general intelligence whose researchers did everything right. Q-learning with tree search, five-layer containment, alignment testing at every stage. Some technical questions become narrative questions.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2024-09-10-the-policy/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Reinforcement-Learning</media:title></media:content><category>fiction</category><category>AI</category><category>AI alignment</category><category>superintelligence</category><category>extrapolated volition</category><category>philosophy</category><category>technology</category><category>society</category><category>politics</category><category>reinforcement learning</category><category>x-risk</category><category>s-risk</category><category>rationality</category></item><item><title>Instrumental Goals and Hidden Codes in RLHF&amp;#39;d Language Models</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2024-03-20-latent-codes/</link><pubDate>Wed, 20 Mar 2024 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2024-03-20-latent-codes/</guid><description>How RLHF-trained language models may develop instrumental goals, and the information-theoretic limits on detecting them.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2024-03-20-latent-codes/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Reinforcement-Learning</media:title></media:content><category>artificial intelligence</category><category>alignment</category><category>reinforcement learning</category><category>RLHF</category><category>deceptive alignment</category></item><item><title>Instrumental Goals and Latent Codes in Reinforcement Learning Fine-tuned Language Models: An Alignment Perspective</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/papers/latent-codes/</link><pubDate>Fri, 15 Mar 2024 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/papers/latent-codes/</guid><description> Open full page Download PDF</description><category>artificial intelligence</category><category>alignment</category><category>reinforcement learning</category></item><item><title>The AI Course: Everything is Utility Maximization</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2024-03-ai-utility-maximization/</link><pubDate>Tue, 12 Mar 2024 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2024-03-ai-utility-maximization/</guid><description>Intelligence as utility maximization under uncertainty. A unifying framework connecting A* search, reinforcement learning, Bayesian networks, and MDPs.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2024-03-ai-utility-maximization/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Reinforcement-Learning</media:title></media:content><category>ai</category><category>reinforcement-learning</category><category>mdp</category><category>utility</category><category>machine-learning</category></item></channel></rss>